    loreSignificance: "Vader's suit is simultaneously the saga's most powerful symbol of fallen greatness and of Palpatine's cruelty. Palpatine designed it to be deliberately painful, deliberately limiting — he wanted Vader perpetually suffering, perpetually dependent, perpetually reminded of his failure. The famous throne room scene in Return of the Jedi — where Luke removes the helmet to reveal Anakin Skywalker's scarred face and the man apologizes to his son — is one of the saga's most emotionally devastating moments precisely because the suit has hidden that humanity for nearly two decades. Anakin dies in his suit, but as himself.",
    description: "Darth Vader's black armor is not a symbol of power — it is a prison. After the lava-banks of Mustafar took most of Anakin Skywalker's body, Palpatine had him rebuilt in black durasteel, a machine of war and a monument to the Emperor's possession of his most powerful weapon. The suit generates Vader's iconic breathing, his chest panel keeping him alive with the precision of a hospital, his helmet sealing away the burned face inside. Anakin Skywalker wears it for twenty-three years, feeling every moment of discomfort, every physical limitation Palpatine engineered into it. It comes off only twice: once in a bacta tank on Mustafar, revealing a helpless ruined man — and once in the Death Star II's throne room, where Luke Skywalker kneels beside his dying father and sees his face for the first time.",
